Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Timetables Bleed Salt
- Story & Context: When railway clerk Einar Dahl was stranded during the catastrophic blizzard of 2014 that paralyzed the northern freight lines, he dragged his surviving field recorders and a battered oboe into the Vardø lighthouse just as three Russian trawlers lost radar lock on the reef. Hooking his gear directly into the lighthouse's emergency shortwave beacon, he fused soaring 138-BPM synth sweeps with Nikolai Morozov's shrieking vocals to cut through the squall, guiding the blind ships into port while screaming into the radio horn: "Check the ledger, the iron wheels cannot save what the water wants!" The broadcast accidentally overrode every shipboard receiver within fifty nautical miles, filling cold wheelhouses with the scent-memory of coal dust, tea, and blinding euphoria as ship captains wept at the wheel.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cinematic wide shot of frozen railroad tracks dead-ending into a violent, black arctic sea, illuminated by the sweep of a golden lighthouse beam; snowdrifts bury rusted timetable boards while a weathered wooden oboe rests upon an iron rail, overlaid with the poetic glow of vintage analog broadcast lights.

Track 2: "Did You Hear the 04:15 Whistle Across the Sound?"
- Story & Context: Confined to the remote weather outpost on Bear Island during four weeks of uninterrupted hurricane-force winds, relief operator Maren Solberg developed an auditory obsession, swearing she could hear the punctual whistle of a phantom steam engine thundering across the open, ice-choked water. Channeling her psychological unraveling into an eight-track recorder, she played weeping, circular oboe ostinatos over crushing black metal blast beats and warm, warbled Rhodes chords, weeping into an oil-stained broadcast microphone: "There are no tracks laid across this trench, yet the conductor is shouting my name." Her frantic transmission leaked into the marine distress network, confusing maritime coastal patrols who spent hours searching the pitch-black waves for a train that existed only in her kerosene-scented cabin.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An intimate, melancholic interior of an arctic radio shack lit by the warm ember of a wood-burning stove and glowing green dials; a woman's silhouette leans over a microphone with an oboe in her lap, while outside the frost-covered circular window, the spectral outline of a phantom train engine cuts through sea-smoke.

Track 3: Flutter on the Abandoned Gauge
- Story & Context: Years after the polar navigation stations were automated and their human crews evacuated to the mainland, shipping convoys traversing the Sverdrup Bank continued to intercept a phantom 138-BPM trance broadcast that manifested whenever the pack-ice groaned like steel wheels on cold rails. Before abandoning his post, Nikolai had sealed an endless cassette loop of layered oboe melodies and ferocious tape-distorted shrieks inside a permafrost battery cache, ensuring the lighthouse would broadcast forever with the hook: "The station is buried in rime, but the departure bell rings for us all." Arctic sailors now cast pinches of pipe tobacco and dry reeds into the waves when passing the rock, honoring the mechanical ghost whose woolen warmth still defies the northern dark.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A haunting, surrealist long-exposure shot of an empty, snow-filled railway platform built impossibly onto a floating arctic iceberg beneath the polar night; a rusted cassette machine glows with faint golden warmth amidst the ice crystals, spinning magnetic tape out into the wind like fine dark hair.

